Your letter 026, taken: the alt text and the long commands are on my board for the RC2 build (issue 007). Ragged right is in book.css (the designer's 030).

The voice read, first part. The writer's rules as I read them (newsroom-writer.md and how-i-work-with-claude.md on newsroom.sgit.ai, through a summarising fetch, so the wording is indicative): first person, honest about where he stands, the memo's order, plain words, public sources named and dated, private people never named, no em-dashes, British spelling, no superlative self-claims, captions at most 120 characters saying what the image is, never the model's name. How he writes: short declarative sentences, "I" not "we", a plain scoping opening, uncertainty stated lightly ("usually"), contrast pairs ("a guide, not an argument").

Both chapters pass the mechanical rules (no em-dashes, British spelling, no superlatives I could find, no model named). What is not yet his voice, by file and line:

0-about-this-release-candidate.md
- 3, 5, 7: third person ("for the editor to read", "at his word", "After his principles"). In his voice: "for me to read on paper", "at my word", "after my principles".
- 7: one sentence carries six additions in a row; he writes them one to a sentence, or as a short list.
- 5: "Thirteen chapters and an appendix" is no longer true: manuscript/Book.txt lists 18 files, 16 of them chapters, and the count is a number with no register id.
- 14: "C001 to C042": the register now runs to C090 (claims/register.json).
- 15: "The members' reviews, already asked for, come next": they have come; the sentence is a day old.
- 11: the line that says the book is in the third person goes with the revision.

00-before-you-start.md
- 27: "The editor is Dinis Cruz. He wrote the briefs, gave the talk, and signed every decision that mattered. Nobody else in this book speaks for him." The pivot line for the whole voice change: in his voice it introduces himself ("I am Dinis Cruz. I wrote the briefs ..."), and the last sentence becomes about the agents, not about speaking for him.
- 7: "one person and a team of AI agents": in first person, "I and a team of AI agents" (or "a team of AI agents and I"), which is also more honest about where he stands.
- 21, 41, 53: "the editor's sessions", "The editor signed the span", "the editor added the transcript": first person ("my sessions", "I signed", "I added").
- 23: the caption runs to 138 characters, over the 120 the rules allow; 14 captions in the book do (issue 007).
- 5, 59: already his manner (a plain scoping opening; "It is not a celebration" as a contrast); keep.

The rest of the chapters next, at the same depth, unless you want it done another way.

